der (docs/readme-checklist.md:4-6) all name the targets as repo-root paths, and the builder copied them into the hrefs as written. Fix direction: write each href in the checklist relative to docs/. Sibling design.md links lose the docs/ prefix. Infrastructure links climb one folder first. The visible link text can stay as the repo path. Change the header sentence at lines 5-6 so it says the links are relative to docs/.","fingerprint":"F6-AC2|docs/readme-checklist.md|repo-root-hrefs-in-docs-subfolder","testable":true,"suggested_test":"Given docs/readme-checklist.md at HEAD, When every relative link is resolved from the file's own folder (GitHub semantics) and each #anchor is checked against the target's GitHub heading slugs, Then 0 links are broken across README.md, docs/design.md, infrastructure/README.md and docs/readme-checklist.md (the orchestrator's gate.write anchors.mjs check; this is a docs gate, not an xUnit class)","untestable_reason":null},{"id":"I2","rule_id":"F6-AC1","layer":"api","files":["README.md","global.json"],"root_cause":"The schema has no docs layer; 'api' is the nearest. README.md:11 says global.json 'pins 10.0.100 and rolls forward within the feature band'. global.json:4 sets \"rollForward\": \"latestFeature\". That setting rolls forward to the newest installed feature band and patch (10.0.2xx, 10.0.3xx and so on), not just within 10.0.1xx. Staying within the band is what 'latestPatch' does. The README describes a different policy from the one the repo pins. Fix direction: change the README wording to match latestFeature (any newer 10.0 feature band is accepted). Do not change global.json to suit the text; that is a toolchain decision for David.","fingerprint":"F6-AC1|README.md|global-json-rollforward-misdescribed","testable":false,"suggested_test":null,"untestable_reason":"The defect is a prose sentence in README.md that misdescribes a JSON setting. No test project reads README prose, and the code being described is already correct. Only reading README.md:11 next to global.json:4 can verify it, for example at review."},{"id":"I3","rule_id":"RENEW-1/2","layer":"api","files":["README.md","src/Uinsure.Policies.Domain/Policies/Policy.cs","docs/requirements.md"],"root_cause":"The schema has no docs layer; 'api' is the nearest. README.md:60 says the renewal window is 'the 30 days up to the end date, inclusive'. The agreed interpretation (docs/requirements.md:90) is 'end − 30 days to the end date, inclusive'. The code matches the rule: Policy.cs:142-144 sets windowStart = LatestTerm.End.AddDays(-RenewalWindowDays) and accepts today from windowStart to windowEnd inclusive, which is 31 calendar days. The README wording reads as 30 inclusive days (end − 29 to end). That is off by one on exactly the boundary an assessor would probe. Fix direction: state the window as the requirement does, from 30 days before the end date to the end date, both inclusive, or give a worked date example.","fingerprint":"RENEW-1/2|README.md|renew-window-off-by-one-wording","testable":false,"suggested_test":null,"untestable_reason":"The behaviour is correct and already covered by the RENEW-1/2 boundary tests (for example tests/Uinsure.Policies.Services.Tests/PolicyServiceRenewWindowTests.cs). The fault is only the README sentence at line 60, and no test reads README prose."},{"id":"I4","rule_id":"F6-AC1","layer":"api","files":["README.md","docs/design.md"],"root_cause":"The schema has no docs layer; 'api' is the nearest. README.md:67 says '409 state conflicts, 422 for every numbered rule'. Several numbered rules are not 422: SELL-1 is 409 (README.md:48 says so), CANCEL-4 is 409 (README.md:58), RENEW-6 is 409 (README.md:62), COULD-2c is 409 (docs/requirements.md:105), and GET-1 is 404. The README contradicts itself and the rule table. docs/design.md:58 says the same thing ('Every numbered rule'), but the 404 and 409 rows above it (design.md:56-57) name the exceptions, so there it is only imprecise. Both lines are reported together here because they share one wording cause. Fix direction: limit the 422 statement to the business rules, meaning every numbered rule that is not a 404 or 409 conflict.","fingerprint":"F6-AC1|README.md|422-every-numbered-rule-overclaim","testable":false,"suggested_test":null,"untestable_reason":"The status mapping is already enforced by the API acceptance tests (409 for SELL-1, CANCEL-4, RENEW-6 and COULD-2c). The defect is the summary sentence at README.md:67 and design.md:58, which no test can read."},{"id":"I5","rule_id":"F6-AC1","layer":"tests","files":["docs/design.md",".githooks/pre-commit",".githooks/commit-msg"],"root_cause":"This is an omission, not a false statement, and it is low severity. The schema has no docs layer; 'tests' is the nearest. docs/design.md:108-112 says the gate 'is the hooks in .githooks/' and lists only pre-commit and pre-push. The folder also holds .githooks/commit-msg, which checks the trailers, and the main-locked marker. design.md:111 describes pre-commit as a privacy scan then a build, but .githooks/pre-commit:9-14 first refuses commits on main once the main-locked marker exists (G8). An assessor comparing the list with the folder would find the extra hook and the missing step. Fix direction: list commit-msg and its job, and mention the main lock in pre-commit (and in pre-push, .githooks/pre-push:9-23), or say that the list is the build and test gates only.","fingerprint":"F6-AC1|docs/design.md|hook-list-incomplete","testable":false,"suggested_test":null,"untestable_reason":"The hooks behave as built. Only the prose list at docs/design.md:108-112 is incomplete, and no test reads it. It can only be checked by comparing the list with the .githooks/ folder at review."}],"previous_attempts":["attempt 1: t1 write a2b43ae, a long README; the checklist links were written README.md#... from docs/ (the same base fault as I1), but the orchestrator's earlier check resolved from the repo root and passed them","attempt 1: David chose a short README wi