+{"schema_version":1,"plan_id":"build","node_id":"F6","attempt":3,"source":"reviewer","source_ref":"docs/trace/build/F6/verdicts/A2-reviewer.json R1, R2, R3, R4; docs/trace/build/F6/verdicts/A2-security.json S1, S2 (round A2, head a797330)","findings":[{"id":"I1","rule_id":"F6-AC1","layer":"api","files":["docs/design.md","docs/qa/build-F2.md","docs/context/20261019-001302-f2-done-merged.md","docs/plans/active/build.dag.yaml","src/Uinsure.Policies.Api/Filters/RejectUnknownQueryParametersFilter.cs","src/Uinsure.Policies.Api/Errors/ValidationProblems.cs","src/Uinsure.Policies.Api/Program.cs","src/Uinsure.Policies.Api/Controllers/PoliciesController.cs","tests/Uinsure.Policies.Api.Tests/RetrieveNotFoundTests.cs"],"root_cause":"Reviewer R1 (P1). The schema has no docs layer, so I used 'api' because the sentence describes API behaviour. Symptom: docs/design.md:64-65 says that ?REFERENCE=x comes back keyed 'reference' and that 'That is the JSON naming policy applied to the key.' Cause: a guess from a QA note was carried forward as fact, and nobody in the chain checked it against the code. WHAT THE REPO PROVES about ?REFERENCE: (a) RejectUnknownQueryParametersFilter.cs:13-17 treats as known only parameters whose binding source is Query, compared case-insensitively. On GET /policies/{reference} the reference is bound from the route, so any query key named reference, in any case, is unknown and gives a 400. A test pins this only for lower case: RetrieveNotFoundTests.cs:84-95 sends ?reference=X and asserts the errors key 'reference'. No test sends REFERENCE. (b) The filter adds the error under the key exactly as the client sent it (RejectUnknownQueryParametersFilter.cs:25). (c) The only key rewrite in the repo is ValidationProblems.cs:15 with :25-26, which lower-cases the first character of each dotted segment and nothing else. On that code alone, 'REFERENCE' would become 'rEFERENCE'. (d) Program.cs:50-56 sets no PropertyNamingPolicy and no DictionaryKeyPolicy, and the comment 